### Suspected cause in the Linux accessibility bridge

In the [tested 3.47.9 source](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/c197ca5199e9ee5661e865596c9c07294dfab51c/engine/src/flutter/shell/platform/linux/fl_view_accessible.cc), `get_node` inserts newly seen semantics IDs into `semantics_nodes_by_id`. That table owns the nodes via `g_object_unref` and is released in `fl_view_accessible_dispose`. I found no pruning of obsolete IDs from that persistent table in the file. `g_hash_table_foreach_remove` in `handle_update_semantics` clears the temporary `pending_children` table instead.

The same retention mechanism is present in [3.32.7](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/3.32.7/engine/src/flutter/shell/platform/linux/fl_view_accessible.cc#L50-L63) and in [master inspected on 2026-11-09](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/635096169b138dd91e73f6a126397bb21aaf48d0/engine/src/flutter/shell/platform/linux/fl_view_accessible.cc). Master has not been runtime-tested for this report.

The source and object-lifetime measurements point to retention of nodes no longer reachable from the active semantics tree. Updates are incremental, so simply deleting all IDs absent from the latest update batch would not be a valid fix. Disabling page semantics removes screen-reader access and is not an acceptable application workaround.

Not tested: long-page scrolling, current master at runtime, normal view disposal, or a second machine. Neither SDK was changed in the original app; the follow-up used isolated SDKs and the standalone sample.

