### Steps to reproduce

1. In an RTL app, put several `Text` paragraphs in a `SelectionArea`. The middle ones are RTL paragraphs whose line starts with an LTR run (`'Hello world שלום עולם'`), ends with one (`'שלום עולם Hello world'`), or is entirely LTR (`'Answer:'`).
2. Mouse-drag from the first paragraph to the last, so the middle paragraphs are covered completely.
3. Look at the highlight / copy the selection.

No custom font is needed. Found in an RTL Hebrew reader app with mixed Hebrew/English text.

### Expected results

Paragraphs that are fully inside the selection are selected completely, as with select-all.

### Actual results

The LTR run at the start (or end) of such a paragraph is left out of the highlight and of the copied text:

```
selected: "ראשון שלום עולםשלום עולם :אחר"
"Hello world שלום עולם" length=21 topRight->11 (start is 0) bottomLeft->21 (end is 21)
"שלום עולם Hello world" length=21 topRight->0 (start is 0) bottomLeft->10 (end is 21)
"Answer:" length=7 topRight->6 (start is 0) bottomLeft->7 (end is 7)
```

Cause: for a selection edge outside the paragraph, `_SelectableFragment._updateSelectionEdge` / `_updateSelectionEdgeByMultiSelectableTextBoundary` pass the edge through `SelectionUtils.adjustDragOffset`, which for RTL returns the visual corner (`topRight` for the start, `bottomLeft` for the end), and then call `paragraph.getPositionForOffset` on that corner. In a bidi line, the visual right end of the first line is not text offset 0: it is the end of the leading LTR run (offset 11 for "Hello world"). In the same way, the visual left end of the last line is the start of the trailing LTR run. LTR paragraphs that start with an RTL run are presumably affected the same way (not verified). The selection edge for an area-1 / area-2 point should probably be `range.start` / `range.end` directly instead of a geometric corner.

