### Steps to reproduce

1. Build a `Semantics(identifier: ...)` node whose identifier changes while nothing else on that node changes (label, flags and actions stay the same).
2. A common real-world case: a `ListView.builder` whose items are not keyed, where each item is `Semantics(identifier: 'row_$name', explicitChildNodes: true, child: ListTile(title: Text(name), onTap: ...))`, and the list re-orders. Each row's element is reused by index: the `Text` label updates, but the container node only gets a new identifier.
3. Inspect the platform accessibility tree (Xcode Accessibility Inspector / XCUITest / Appium / Maestro on iOS; `uiautomator dump` resource-id on Android), or run the test below, which records what the framework sends to the engine.

### Expected results

The platform node carries the new identifier, as the framework's semantics tree does (`tester.getSemantics(...)`, Widget Inspector).

### Actual results

The engine never receives the new identifier: the platform node keeps the old one. After a re-order the identifiers no longer match the rows they are on (row 1 shows `B` but has id `row_A`), so a UI test that selects by identifier taps the wrong row.

Cause: `SemanticsNode.updateWith` assigns `_identifier = config.identifier`, but `_isDifferentFromCurrentSemanticAnnotation` does not compare `identifier`, so the node is not marked dirty and `SemanticsOwner.sendSemanticsUpdate` skips it. When the label also changes on the same node, the update goes out and carries the new identifier, which is likely why this looks fine in most cases.

#165562 reported the same symptom and was closed after a check in the Widget Inspector. The inspector reads the framework tree, which is correct. The engine side is not. On `master` today `_isDifferentFromCurrentSemanticAnnotation` still has no `identifier` check.

Possible fix: add `_identifier != config.identifier` to `_isDifferentFromCurrentSemanticAnnotation`, as #185707 did for `customSemanticsActions`.

Related: #195755 is a similar stale-semantics bug for `TextSpan.semanticsIdentifier` and `semanticsLabel`, at another layer: there `TextSpan.compareTo` skips the update, so the framework tree is stale too. Its fix (#195756) does not touch `SemanticsNode`.

