Shift+Arrow Left/Right go through `_granularlyExtendSelection` and hit the same line.

`SliverChildBuilderDelegate` keeps the selected items alive, but they are no longer painted. `MultiSelectableSelectionContainerDelegate._getSelectionGeometry` gets a non-finite point for them, so the region's geometry still has a selection (`hasSelection` is true), but `startSelectionPoint` and `endSelectionPoint` are null. `_granularlyExtendSelection` and `_directionallyExtendSelection` check only `hasSelection`, and `_determineIsAdjustingSelectionEnd` then dereferences both points with `!`. `_directionallyExtendSelection` also dereferences the chosen edge with `!`. The same happens when only one edge is scrolled away.

We see this in production in a Hebrew reader app: users select a passage, scroll on, and press Shift+Arrow.

