### Expected result and verification

Frame state used during presentation should remain available until the last renderer reference is released. Moving `task_runner` and `frame_mutex` cleanup to `finalize()` makes the regression pass 100 consecutive repetitions; the complete Linux unit suite passes 862 tests.

Related: #188659 / #188702 and the open #193533. The latter addresses OpenGL/Subsurface renderer lifetime; this report isolates the software renderer, whose file is not changed by that PR.

Prepared with AI assistance; the failing baseline, symbolized stack, fixed regression and complete Linux suite were executed locally.

### Steps to reproduce

1. `flutter create bug`, replace `lib/main.dart` with the code sample, run `flutter build web`, and serve `build/web`.
2. Open it in Chrome on an Android tablet with at least a 10" display and 8 GB of RAM, such as a Pixel Tablet or a Galaxy Tab S11, held in landscape. Chrome loads the desktop site there by default (https://developer.chrome.com/blog/desktop-mode). On a phone, tick ⋮ > Desktop site.
3. Read the AppBar, then tap Email.
4. Close the keyboard and tap Low field.

### Expected results

`ui_web.browser.operatingSystem` is `android`, as Safari's desktop site on an iPad is detected as iOS (flutter/engine#25957). Email opens the email keyboard with a Next key, the view keeps its size and reports the keyboard in `viewInsets.bottom`, and Low field scrolls up above the keyboard.

### Actual results

`operatingSystem` is `linux` and the AppBar shows `TargetPlatform.linux`, so:

- The engine sets `inputmode` and `enterkeyhint` only on iOS and Android, so Gboard and Samsung Keyboard show their text layout with the action key labelled Go. Go still moves focus here; inside an `AutofillGroup`, Next does not (#195741).
- `isMobile` is false, so the view shrinks under the keyboard instead of reporting an inset, and because `EditableText` reveals the caret only when the inset grows, Low field takes focus out of sight until the first keystroke.
- `ThemeData` takes the linux defaults, `VisualDensity.compact` and `MaterialTapTargetSize.shrinkWrap`.

Release (dart2js) build of the sample, as is and with the workaround below:

| Measured                              | As is                                     | With the workaround                       |
|---------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|
| AppBar                                | `TargetPlatform.linux`                    | `TargetPlatform.android`                  |
| Email `inputmode` / `enterkeyhint`    | none / none                               | `email` / `next`                          |
| Keyboard on Email                     | text layout, action key Go                | email layout with @, action key Next      |
| View with keyboard up, Pixel Tablet   | 1280 x 668 shrinks to 1280 x 332, inset 0 | stays 1280 x 668, `viewInsets.bottom` 336 |
| View with keyboard up, Galaxy Tab S11 | 1205 x 579 shrinks to 1205 x 270, inset 0 | stays 1205 x 579, `viewInsets.bottom` 309 |
| Low field tapped                      | focused, but below the visible view       | scrolled up to the keyboard               |

Unticking Desktop site on the same tablets gives `android`, the email keyboard and a working Next. On these tablets the desktop site is Chrome's default rather than something the user asked for, and the app cannot adapt around it: `inputmode`, `enterkeyhint` and the keyboard inset are engine decisions keyed to the detected OS, with no app-facing setting other than the testing override used below.

Browser values, the same on both tablets apart from the Chrome version:

```text
navigator.userAgent       Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/157.0.0.0 Safari/537.36
navigator.platform        Linux armv81
navigator.maxTouchPoints  5
media queries             (pointer: coarse) and (hover: none) match
navigator.userAgentData   platform "Linux", mobile false; high-entropy architecture "x86", model ""
```

`detectOperatingSystem` finds Android only by "Android" in the user agent, but `navigator.platform` keeps Android's value: Chrome freezes it to `Linux armv81` on Android and to `Linux x86_64` on Linux and ChromeOS whatever the CPU (https://www.chromium.org/updates/ua-reduction/), and Firefox does the same. So a check like the iPad one, `platform == 'Linux armv81' && maxTouchPoints > 0` ahead of the Linux branch, identifies these tablets without flipping ARM Linux or ChromeOS devices.

#147346 (3.24) switched the framework to the engine's detection and so dropped the framework's own fallback from #61587 (2020), `(pointer: fine)` or else Android, which until then gave these tablets `TargetPlatform.android` (the engine already said linux).

Workaround for others: set `ui_web.browser.debugOperatingSystemOverride = ui_web.OperatingSystem.android` first thing in `main` when that check matches (code below). Release builds honor it, and it fixed everything in the table.

Related: #195741 (Next in an `AutofillGroup`), #180144 (`SearchAnchor` closes a non-full-screen view when `MediaQuery.size` changes, which here happens whenever the keyboard rises).

