### Steps to reproduce

An engine that runs Dart before any Activity exists is common for media apps: a `MediaBrowserService` starts a cached `FlutterEngine` when Android Auto (or another media controller) binds to it. In that engine, any `SystemChrome`, `HapticFeedback`, `SystemSound` or `Clipboard` call returns a future that never completes. This is still the case after an Activity later attaches to the same engine.

We hit this when `main` awaited `SystemChrome.setPreferredOrientations` before `runApp`. With Android Auto starting the app, `main` stopped there. Android Auto waited on the app indefinitely, and when the user then opened the app on the phone, it stayed on the launch screen. There is no error or log line in either case.

To reproduce with the code sample below:

1. `flutter create --platforms android --empty repro`, then add the two Kotlin classes, the receiver entry and `lib/main.dart` from the code sample.
2. `flutter build apk --debug`, then `adb install -r build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/app-debug.apk`.
3. Start the engine with no Activity: `adb shell am broadcast -n dev.repro.headless_chrome/.HeadlessReceiver`.
4. After a few seconds, attach an Activity to the same engine: `adb shell am start -n dev.repro.headless_chrome/.MainActivity`.
5. Look at `adb logcat -s flutter`.

### Expected results

Each call completes, as a no-op or with an error, when there is no Activity to apply it to. A call on a channel that has no handler at all already fails at once with a `MissingPluginException`, so awaiting it is safe. Awaiting a `flutter/platform` call should be safe in the same way.

### Actual results

The four `flutter/platform` calls never complete, and stay pending after the Activity attaches. A new call made after the Activity attaches completes normally.

The cause seems to be in `PlatformChannel.parsingMethodCallHandler`, which returns without calling `result` when no `PlatformMessageHandler` is set:

```java
if (platformMessageHandler == null
    && !method.equals("SystemNavigator.setFrameworkHandlesBack")) {
  // If no explicit PlatformMessageHandler has been registered then we don't
  // need to forward this call to an API. Return.
  return;
}
```

#192286 recently added the `setFrameworkHandlesBack` exception, storing the value and replaying it when a handler attaches. Every other method still gets no reply.

`KeyboardChannel` already answers in the same situation (flutter/engine#42758). `TextInputChannel`, `LocalizationChannel`, `MouseCursorChannel` and a few other system channels have the same early `return`; I have not tested those.

Calling `result.success(null)` in that branch would let Dart continue. For `setPreferredOrientations` and `setSystemUIOverlayStyle`, storing the last value and applying it when a `PlatformPlugin` attaches, as #192286 does for back handling, would also keep the effect. I'm happy to send a PR for either if that direction is fine.

