Built-in Retina display (2x, 120 Hz), viewport 1728 x 936 CSS pixels. Release builds. Median of 3 runs. CPU is the percentage of one core.

| Build | Scenario | fps | Raster ms/frame | Build ms/frame | Tab CPU | GPU process CPU |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| CSS | spinning `div` | – | – | – | 0.9% | 2.2% |
| JS (CanvasKit) | idle, cards | 0 | – | – | 0.0% | 0.0% |
| JS (CanvasKit) | spinning | 120 | 0.11 | 0.15 | 8.6% | 9.9% |
| JS (CanvasKit) | spinning, cards | 120 | 1.16 | 0.41 | 33.0% | 25.8% |
| Wasm (skwasm) | spinning | 120 | 0.26 | 0.24 | 17.2% | 18.4% |
| Wasm (skwasm) | spinning, cards | 120 | 1.19 | 0.19 | 29.4% | 20.9% |
| Wasm (skwasm), COOP/COEP | spinning, cards | 120 | 1.30 | 0.11 | 79.6% | 13.6% |

On an external 1080p display (1x, 60 Hz), raster time per frame also grows with the cards (JS: 0.39 to 0.69 ms, Wasm: 0.19 to 0.64 ms). At 60 Hz and 1x, the CPU differences were within the run-to-run noise.

How we measured: a fresh Chrome profile driven through the DevTools protocol. Each run opens the page in its own browser context, so it gets its own renderer process. After a 10 s warm-up, CPU time of the page's renderer process and of the GPU process is read with `SystemInfo.getProcessInfo` in six 2 s intervals, and the median interval is used. Raster and build times are the `FrameTiming` values the sample exposes. CPU time on Apple Silicon varies between runs (performance vs. efficiency cores), so single runs differ. The direction was the same in every run on the Retina display.

The multi-threaded skwasm row is probably dominated by #196484.

