### Use case

We build a live shopping app. A live HLS stream with a DVR window (e.g. the last hour) shows products one after another. The products come from our backend with absolute start/end times (UTC). Users can:
• jump back to a product they missed ("seek to the product that started at 10:42:13 UTC"),
• see markers for all products on the seek bar,
• see which product is shown at the current playback position.
All of this needs a mapping between the player position and the wall-clock time of the stream. HLS provides exactly this via #EXT-X-PROGRAM-DATE-TIME, and both platform players already expose it — but video_player does not.

### Proposal

A small, optional API on VideoPlayerController (and the platform interface), for example:
/// The wall-clock time of the media at the current position, if the stream
/// provides one (HLS EXT-X-PROGRAM-DATE-TIME, DASH availabilityStartTime, ...).
/// Null if unknown, e.g. for most VOD files.
Future<DateTime?> get programDateTime;

/// The wall-clock time range that can currently be seeked to (DVR window),
/// or null if unknown.
Future<DateTimeRange?> get seekableProgramDateRange;

/// Seeks to the given wall-clock time within the seekable range.
Future<void> seekToProgramDateTime(DateTime dateTime);
Alternatives that would also solve it:
• add DateTime? programDateTime to VideoPlayerValue, updated together with position (no extra platform calls), or
• a lower-level escape hatch: an official, supported way to obtain the native player (ExoPlayer / AVPlayer) for a given controller, so apps can implement such features without relying on private fields.
Platform implementation sketch:
• Android: Timeline.Window window = player.getCurrentTimeline().getWindow(player.getCurrentMediaItemIndex(), new Timeline.Window()); → window.windowStartTimeMs != C.TIME_UNSET ? window.windowStartTimeMs + player.getCurrentPosition() : null; seekable range from windowStartTimeMs and window.durationUs.
• iOS/macOS: player.currentItem?.currentDate(); seekable range from seekableTimeRanges mapped via currentDate() - (currentTime - range.start); seeking via AVPlayerItem.seek(to: Date).
• Web: optional / null initially.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Run the code sample in profile mode on an Android device with Impeller (Vulkan).
   - For GPU time, add the manifest meta-data `io.flutter.embedding.android.EnableVulkanGPUTracing` = `true` and capture a systrace. Impeller then writes a `GPUTracer` counter (GPU ms per frame).
2. The app shows six small (80×80) blurred panels in one `BackdropGroup`, over a background that moves every frame. Each panel's filter is slightly different, so the group takes the per-filter path.
3. Every 5 seconds it switches between three filter kinds and prints raster p50 and p90 for the previous one:
   - `blurOnly`: `ImageFilter.blur` (sigma differs per panel by 0.01);
   - `colorMatrixOuter`: `ImageFilter.compose(outer: ColorFilter.matrix(alpha a), inner: blur)`;
   - `blurOuter`: `ImageFilter.compose(outer: blur, inner: ColorFilter.matrix(alpha a))`.
4. `colorMatrixOuter` and `blurOuter` draw the same picture: scaling premultiplied pixels by `a` commutes with a linear blur.

### Expected results

The two compose orders cost about the same, because each panel only needs its own 80×80 area plus the blur padding.

### Actual results

Measured on an OPPO CPH2841, Android 17, Snapdragon SM8850 / Adreno 840, Impeller Vulkan, 1440×3168. The app ran at 60 Hz.

**GPU time per frame** (Impeller `GPUTracer` counter, profile build with `EnableVulkanGPUTracing`; 2-3 windows of 5 s per mode, the first 0.3 s after each switch dropped):

| Mode | Frames | GPU p50 | GPU p90 | Mean |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| blurOnly | 554 | 7.20 ms | 8.69 ms | 7.40 ms |
| **colorMatrixOuter** | 831 | **7.74 ms** | **9.43 ms** | 7.78 ms |
| **blurOuter** | 730 | **6.01 ms** | **7.57 ms** | 6.60 ms |

- `colorMatrixOuter` costs **+1.73 ms GPU p50 (+29 %)** over `blurOuter` for the same picture, about 0.29 ms per panel.
- Per-window p50s were steady: colorMatrixOuter 7.76 / 7.75 / 7.66; blurOuter 6.01 / 6.01 / 5.87.
- **CPU raster time** (the printed p50/p90, profile build without GPU tracing, two cycles) shows no such difference: blurOnly 2.97-2.98 / 3.05-3.08 ms, colorMatrixOuter 2.99-3.00 / 3.06-3.07 ms, blurOuter 3.19-3.20 / 3.26-3.28 ms. The cost is GPU work.
- `blurOuter` came out cheaper than `blurOnly`. I have not explained that.
- I did not take a GPU frame capture (AGI or RenderDoc) showing the downsample target size per panel. So "the whole screen" in the cause below comes from the source, and the GPU numbers above agree with it.

**Cause (engine source).**
- For a backdrop, `Entity::Render` gives the outermost filter a coverage hint the size of the layer:
  - 3.47.9: [`impeller/entity/entity.cc#L145-L156`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/c197ca5199e9ee5661e865596c9c07294dfab51c/engine/src/flutter/impeller/entity/entity.cc#L145-L156)
- A Gaussian blur on the outside uses that hint and asks its input only for the hinted area:
  - [`gaussian_blur_filter_contents.cc#L825-L841`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/c197ca5199e9ee5661e865596c9c07294dfab51c/engine/src/flutter/impeller/entity/contents/filters/gaussian_blur_filter_contents.cc#L825-L841)
- `compose` wraps the inner filter's contents as the outer filter's input:
  - [`impeller/display_list/image_filter.cc#L94-L110`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/c197ca5199e9ee5661e865596c9c07294dfab51c/engine/src/flutter/impeller/display_list/image_filter.cc#L94-L110)
- When the outer filter is a color matrix, `ColorMatrixFilterContents::RenderFilter` calls `inputs[0]->GetSnapshot("ColorMatrix", renderer, entity)` with no coverage limit:
  - [`color_matrix_filter_contents.cc#L46`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/c197ca5199e9ee5661e865596c9c07294dfab51c/engine/src/flutter/impeller/entity/contents/filters/color_matrix_filter_contents.cc#L46)
- So the inner blur's `RenderToSnapshot` runs with no hint and processes the whole backdrop texture.
- 3.50.0 is identical:
  - [`color_matrix_filter_contents.cc#L46`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/ab2912035e160d9cad6306df307576da6128ebdb/engine/src/flutter/impeller/entity/contents/filters/color_matrix_filter_contents.cc#L46)
  - [`gaussian_blur_filter_contents.cc#L828-L844`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/ab2912035e160d9cad6306df307576da6128ebdb/engine/src/flutter/impeller/entity/contents/filters/gaussian_blur_filter_contents.cc#L828-L844)
  - `master` on 2026-11-16: the same.
- The other color filter contents probably share this pattern. I checked only the matrix one.

**Possible fix.** Have color filter contents pass the coverage hint, or the coverage limit they were given, through to `GetSnapshot` of their input. A color filter is per-pixel, so the area it needs from its input equals its output area.

**Why it matters.** Composing an alpha (or saturation) color matrix with a blur is a common way to fade a backdrop blur, or to tint it like iOS vibrancy (`CupertinoPopupSurface` composes a saturation matrix with its blur). Related, different path: [#194832](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/issues/194832) (runtime-effect compose).

Related: #196725 (the same color matrix filter samples its input with nearest-neighbour filtering).

### Code sample

```dart
import 'dart:async';
import 'dart:ui' as ui;

import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:flutter/scheduler.dart';

void main() =>
    runApp(const MaterialApp(debugShowCheckedModeBanner: false, home: Repro()));

enum Mode { blurOnly, colorMatrixOuter, blurOuter }

class Repro extends StatefulWidget {
  const Repro({super.key});

  @override
  State<Repro> createState() => _ReproState();
}

class _ReproState extends State<Repro> with SingleTickerProviderStateMixin {
  late final AnimationController _move = AnimationController(
    vsync: this,
    duration: const Duration(seconds: 4),
  )..repeat();
  late final Timer _cycle;
  final List<FrameTiming> _timings = <FrameTiming>[];
  Mode _mode = Mode.blurOnly;

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();
    SchedulerBinding.instance.addTimingsCallback(_onTimings);
    _cycle = Timer.periodic(const Duration(seconds: 5), (_) => _next());
  }

  @override
  void dispose() {
    _cycle.cancel();
    SchedulerBinding.instance.removeTimingsCallback(_onTimings);
    _move.dispose();
    super.dispose();
  }

  void _onTimings(List<FrameTiming> timings) => _timings.addAll(timings);

  void _next() {
    final raster =
        _timings.map((t) => t.rasterDuration.inMicroseconds / 1000.0).toList()
          ..sort();
    if (raster.isNotEmpty) {
      double p(double q) => raster[((raster.length - 1) * q).round()];
      debugPrint(
        '${_mode.name}: frames=${raster.length} '
        'raster p50=${p(0.5).toStringAsFixed(2)} ms '
        'p90=${p(0.9).toStringAsFixed(2)} ms',
      );
    }
    _timings.clear();
    setState(() => _mode = Mode.values[(_mode.index + 1) % Mode.values.length]);
  }

  ui.ImageFilter _filter(int i) {
    final alpha = 0.5 + i * 0.05; // a different filter per panel
    final blur = ui.ImageFilter.blur(
      sigmaX: 20 + i * 0.01,
      sigmaY: 20 + i * 0.01,
      tileMode: TileMode.mirror,
