### Steps to reproduce

1. Run the code sample below in release mode on an Android device that uses Impeller (Vulkan).
2. The screen shows two blurred panels over a striped background.
   - Both are `BackdropFilter.grouped` with the same `ImageFilter.blur` inside one `BackdropGroup`.
   - The right panel ("fading") sits in a `FadeTransition` that runs 0 → 1 → 0 on repeat (2 s each way).
3. Watch the right panel.
4. Tap the button to switch to plain (ungrouped) `BackdropFilter`s and watch again.

### Expected results

In both modes, the right panel fades in and out smoothly: its blur, its tint and its label all follow the `FadeTransition` opacity.

### Actual results

**Grouped (two equal filters):** the right panel never fades. Its blur, its tint and its label stay at full strength for the whole animation, the same as the static panel.

**Plain:** it fades as expected.

Measured from 10-s screen recordings at 30 fps (300 frames per mode). Each frame's sharpness inside the fading panel was taken as the standard deviation of luma, on a scale from 0 (as blurred as the static panel) to 1 (as sharp as the unblurred stripes):

| Mode | Frames at 0-0.05 (fully blurred) | Frames in between | Range |
|---|---|---|---|
| grouped | **300 / 300** | 0 | 0.00-0.00 |
| plain | 21 / 300 | 279 | 0.00-0.94, a smooth triangle wave in step with the fade |

At opacity exactly 0 the `OpacityLayer` isn't painted at all, so the grouped panel should also blink out for single frames. None of the 300 recorded frames caught that.

**Cause (from reading the engine source).**

1. `OpacityLayer` hands its opacity down when its children can take it (`flow/layers/opacity_layer.cc`, `set_children_can_accept_opacity`). `BackdropFilterLayer` reports `kSaveLayerRenderFlags`, so it can. The opacity becomes the alpha of the backdrop `saveLayer`'s paint.
2. In `impeller::Canvas::SaveLayer`, a backdrop whose `backdrop_id` is shared by two or more layers with equal filters takes a fast path. It draws the shared filtered snapshot with only `paint.blend_mode`, then does a plain `Save` and returns. **`paint.color.alpha` is never applied**, and the layer's children get no alpha either. Neither is the stack's `distributed_opacity`.
3. The normal path applies the alpha a few lines further down (`paint_copy.color.alpha *= ...`) and composites the subpass with it on `Restore`.

Source links:
- 3.47.9, fast path ignores alpha: [`canvas.cc#L1858-L1891`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/c197ca5199e9ee5661e865596c9c07294dfab51c/engine/src/flutter/impeller/display_list/canvas.cc#L1858-L1891) (blend mode only at L1886, `Save(0); return;` at L1889-1890)
- 3.47.9, normal path applies alpha: [`canvas.cc#L1895-L1897`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/c197ca5199e9ee5661e865596c9c07294dfab51c/engine/src/flutter/impeller/display_list/canvas.cc#L1895-L1897)
- 3.50.0, the same (now `Save(total_content_depth)`): [`canvas.cc#L1980-L2016`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/ab2912035e160d9cad6306df307576da6128ebdb/engine/src/flutter/impeller/display_list/canvas.cc#L1980-L2016) and [`#L2020-L2022`](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/ab2912035e160d9cad6306df307576da6128ebdb/engine/src/flutter/impeller/display_list/canvas.cc#L2020-L2022)
- `master` on 2026-11-16: the same.

It follows from the code that a group holding only one filter takes the normal path and fades correctly. That case was not recorded in this run.

**Possible fix.** When the layer's paint, or the pending `distributed_opacity`, carries alpha < 1 (or a color or image filter), do one of these:
- apply it to the drawn snapshot and keep it for the children;
- or skip the fast path for that layer and take the normal subpass path, reusing the cached `texture_slot`, so no extra backdrop flip is needed.

**Why it matters.** `BackdropFilter.grouped` is the documented way to make several blurred surfaces cheap, and fading one of them is the most common animation on such surfaces. Today the only workarounds are:
- an alpha color matrix composed into the filter, which makes the filters unequal and loses the sharing (see the separate compose issues);
- or moving the fading surface out of the group.

The draft PR [#192596](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/pull/192596) (automatic backdrop groups) would make this hit any app that fades sibling `BackdropFilter`s. Related, older and different mechanism: [#31706](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/issues/31706).

