### Steps to reproduce

1. Use an Android 12+ device whose emoji font has been updated by Google Play services. You can check with:
   ```
   adb shell cmd font dump | grep -A1 NotoColorEmoji.ttf
   ```
   An updated font shows a path under `/data/fonts/files/…` and `Font is updated from /system/fonts/NotoColorEmoji.ttf`. Phones that receive Google's Noto 3D emoji through Play services (for example Motorola on Android 17) are in this state.

   Emulator alternative (what I used): an Android 17.0 `google_apis` arm64 image, whose system `NotoColorEmoji.ttf` is rev 2.051. I took `/system/fonts/NotoColorEmoji.ttf` from the Android 17.2 Google Play image (rev 3.052, Noto 3D) and installed it as a font update the same way Play services does: `adb root`, `cmd font install-debug-cert <cert.der>` (the cert must be readable by system_server, e.g. under `/data/system`), then `cmd font update NotoColorEmoji.ttf NotoColorEmoji.ttf.fsv_sig` with an fs-verity PKCS#7 signature made with that cert.
2. Run the code sample below. It shows the same emoji in a Flutter `Text` and in a native `TextView`.

### Expected results

Flutter draws emoji with the same font the platform uses, like the `TextView`, notifications and the keyboard do.

### Actual results

Flutter keeps using the font listed in the system image's `/system/etc/fonts.xml`, and fonts updated in `/data/fonts` are never seen:

- With Noto 3D delivered as an update, the `TextView` shows the 3D emoji and Flutter shows the older flat ones (screenshot).
- On devices whose system image is older than the update, emoji added by the update render as tofu in Flutter, while native views and notifications render them.

The engine creates its font manager with `SkFontMgr_New_Android(nullptr, SkFontScanner_Make_FreeType())` in `engine/src/flutter/txt/src/txt/platform_android.cc`, which only parses `fonts.xml`. Since Android 12, updated system fonts are registered by FontManagerService under `/data/fonts/files` (readable by all apps) and are listed by `android.graphics.fonts.SystemFonts.getAvailableFonts()` and the NDK `ASystemFontIterator` (https://source.android.com/docs/core/fonts/custom-font-fallback). Related: #93881.

The workaround available to apps today is risky. It reads the updated font through `SystemFonts.getAvailableFonts()` and registers it with `FontLoader`/`loadFontFromList`, which copies the whole file into memory. Noto 3D's `NotoColorEmoji.ttf` (rev 3.052) is 215,029,220 bytes of `sbix` bitmaps, so the copy through a platform channel ends in `OutOfMemoryError` on phones with a 384 MB Java heap limit. Native apps pay nothing for the same font because the platform memory-maps it.

