### Steps to reproduce

1. `flutter create --platforms=linux multiview_repro`, replace `lib/main.dart` and add the runner code from the code sample. It opens a second `GtkWindow` with its own `FlView`, so its own engine, `REPRO_SECOND_AFTER` seconds after start.
2. `flutter build linux --release`
3. On X11: `GDK_BACKEND=x11 REPRO_SECOND_AFTER=3 build/linux/x64/release/bundle/multiview_repro`
4. The same with `REPRO_CLEAR_GL=1`, which calls `gdk_gl_context_clear_current()` right before `fl_view_new()`.

### Expected results

The second window shows "view: second" on orange.

### Actual results

In step 3 the app logs `Failed to setup compositor shaders, unable to make OpenGL context current` when the second view is realized, and the second window shows orange without the text. In step 4 there is no warning and the text shows.

What seems to happen:
- GTK 3 draws with GLX on X11. Once a window has been painted, GDK leaves a GLX context current on the GTK thread.
- When the second view is realized, `setup_shader()` in `fl_compositor_opengl_shader.cc` calls `fl_opengl_manager_make_platform_current()`, an `eglMakeCurrent` on that thread. libglvnd does not make an EGL context current while a GLX one is current ("Another window API already has a current context"), so the shaders are never set up.

Wayland is not affected: GDK draws with EGL there.

This was found with one engine per window, since multiple windows are not available on stable. On master the shaders are still set up on the GTK thread when the view renderer is realized (`fl_view_renderer_opengl_realize` → `fl_compositor_opengl_new`), and since #192137 the draw handler leaves its GL context current on purpose. So a view realized after another one has drawn, including another view of the same engine, looks like it would fail the same way. Not tried on master.

