### Analysis

**CameraX root cause.** The plugin starts a persistent recording. When the
screen goes off, the camera closes, and the Recorder's
`getReadyToReleaseFuture()` callback calls `requestReset(...)`. For a persistent
recording, `requestReset` calls `resetVideo()`. That sets
`mVideoEncoder = null`, but the Recorder stays `RECORDING`. A stop that arrives
after this crashes at `mVideoEncoder.stop(...)` in `stopInternal`
(`Recorder.java:2409`). It has already set `mInProgressRecordingStopping`, so
the recording never finalizes. If the stop reaches the Recorder's executor
before that callback, the recording stops normally. That is why the delay makes
the bug much more frequent.

CameraX fixes:

- The NPE is fixed in camera-video 1.7.0-alpha02 by
  [77c58acf](https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/support/+/77c58acfff482000d9d5e1cecf8774cc20772cb6),
  which adds a null check.
- The missing `Finalize` is fixed only on androidx-main, by
  [7b541c10](https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/support/+/7b541c103f7ff50274cd42f31b96d44d318aaf4b).
  It is not in 1.7.0-rc02.

With CameraX 1.6.2, which the plugin pins, the stop can wait forever.

**Plugin: no timeout.**
[`stopVideoRecording`](https://github.com/flutter/packages/blob/faab724e57d881c8ac9ec33bdf86b418cf0822df/packages/camera/camera_android_camerax/lib/src/android_camera_camerax.dart#L1294-L1299)
waits for `Finalize` with no time limit:

```dart
    /// Stop the active recording and wait for the video recording to be finalized.
    await recording!.close();
    VideoRecordEvent event = await videoRecordingEventStreamQueue.next;
    while (event is! VideoRecordEventFinalize) {
      event = await videoRecordingEventStreamQueue.next;
    }
```

**Plugin: shared queue field.** All recordings share one broadcast
`StreamController`, and each wait loop reads the queue from an instance field
([L106-L111](https://github.com/flutter/packages/blob/faab724e57d881c8ac9ec33bdf86b418cf0822df/packages/camera/camera_android_camerax/lib/src/android_camera_camerax.dart#L106-L111)):

```dart
  /// Stream queue to pick up finalized video recording events in
  /// [stopVideoRecording].
  @visibleForTesting
  StreamQueue<VideoRecordEvent> videoRecordingEventStreamQueue = StreamQueue<VideoRecordEvent>(
    videoRecordingEventStreamController.stream,
  );
```

`startVideoCapturing` replaces the queue in that field
([L1206-L1209](https://github.com/flutter/packages/blob/faab724e57d881c8ac9ec33bdf86b418cf0822df/packages/camera/camera_android_camerax/lib/src/android_camera_camerax.dart#L1206-L1209)):

```dart
    unawaited(videoRecordingEventStreamQueue.cancel());
    videoRecordingEventStreamQueue = StreamQueue<VideoRecordEvent>(
      videoRecordingEventStreamController.stream,
    );
```

Here is how the waiting stop moves to the next recording's queue:

1. `dispose()` sets `recording = null`
   ([L535-L538](https://github.com/flutter/packages/blob/faab724e57d881c8ac9ec33bdf86b418cf0822df/packages/camera/camera_android_camerax/lib/src/android_camera_camerax.dart#L535-L538)),
   so a new recording can start while the old stop is still waiting.
2. The new start calls `cancel()` on the old queue without `immediate`. That
   cancel waits for the old stop's pending `next`. The next native event, for
   example the new recording's `Start`, completes that pending `next`.
3. The old loop then calls `videoRecordingEventStreamQueue.next` again, and the
   field now holds the new recording's queue. From then on, the old stop and
   the new recording's stop take turns at the same queue. Whichever is next in
   line when `Finalize` arrives gets it.
4. If the old stop gets it, it returns `XFile(videoOutputPath!)`, the new
   recording's file. It also sets `recording = null` and unbinds `videoCapture`.
   The new recording's stop then waits forever.
5. The run where the second stop won, as described above, fits this
   turn-taking. We have not confirmed that this is the reason.

Issue #183880 reported the same CameraX NPE after backgrounding. It was closed
by flutter/packages#12145, which clears the Dart recording state on
`dispose()`. That change is already in 0.7.6+1, and the stop can still wait
forever. Issue #182960, which is open, asks to surface `Finalize` errors. The
timeout below would produce such an error.

### Suggested plugin-side fixes

1. Give each recording its own subscription. `startVideoCapturing` would
   create the queue or subscription for this recording and keep it with the
   recording. Then `stopVideoRecording` waits only on that one and cancels it
   when done, instead of reading the shared `videoRecordingEventStreamQueue`
   field on each step.
2. Add a timeout to the `Finalize` wait in `stopVideoRecording`. When it
   expires, clear `recording` and `pendingRecording`, unbind `videoCapture`,
   and throw a `CameraException` such as
   `videoRecordingFailed: the recording was not finalized`. Then the app gets
   an error instead of a future that never completes.
3. Treat a `Finalize` with an error as a failed recording. In 0.7.6+1 this
   already holds for the recording's own `Finalize`. With (1), a stop can no
   longer receive another recording's `Finalize`.
4. Consider moving to a CameraX version that contains 7b541c10 once it is
   released.

### Steps to reproduce

Reproduction re-run and report prepared with the help of an AI coding assistant; all results verified on my machine.

This is the bug #193439 reported (multi-threaded skwasm: `SkMutex` is a no-op
under `-sWASM_WORKERS` without `-pthread`, so main-thread SkParagraph layout
and the raster worker race on one `FT_Face`). That issue was closed by the
bot and locked, with a comment reporting it still reproduced on 3.49.0-0.2.pre;
the bot asked finders to file a new issue referencing it. Filing as requested:
it **still reproduces on stable 3.50.0**, and we also hit what looks like the
same corruption in a production app with a different trap message.

Repro (unchanged from #193439, run literally):

