### Analysis

`RenderParagraph` splits its selectables into `_SelectableFragment`s at each placeholder. `_SelectableFragment._handleSelectParagraph` handles a paragraph that spans several fragments correctly (`_handleSelectMultiFragmentTextBoundary` returns `SelectionResult.next` / `previous`). The problem is in the container:

`MultiSelectableSelectionContainerDelegate._handleSelectBoundary` (used by `handleSelectParagraph`) only dispatches the event to selectables whose bounding boxes contain the pointer position. When the clicked fragment returns `SelectionResult.next`, the next fragment does not contain the position, so the loop ends the selection there (`currentSelectionStartIndex = currentSelectionEndIndex = index - 1; return SelectionResult.end;`). Fragments before the clicked one never receive the event at all.

`Text` avoids this with its private `_SelectableTextContainerDelegate`, which overrides `handleSelectParagraph` to walk every fragment of the same paragraph. A `RichText` that registers directly with an ancestor `SelectionArea` gets the generic delegate and the broken behavior. That is the common case for third-party rich-text renderers. Perhaps the multi-fragment paragraph logic could live in `MultiSelectableSelectionContainerDelegate`, or `RichText` could wrap its fragments the same way `Text` does.

Word selection (double-click) is not affected, because a word never crosses a placeholder.

