### Steps to reproduce

On Flutter web, a value the browser autofills into the field that was focused when the autofill form went dormant is thrown away when that field is focused again. Values autofilled into the other fields of the group are kept.

This happens with password managers that ask the user to authenticate before filling. The prompt takes focus away from the page, so the engine closes the text input connection and the autofill form goes dormant. The manager then fills the dormant form.

Minimal app:

import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() => runApp(const MaterialApp(home: Scaffold(body: LoginForm())));

class LoginForm extends StatelessWidget {
  const LoginForm({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Center(
      child: SizedBox(
        width: 320,
        child: AutofillGroup(
          child: Column(
            mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min,
            children: const [
              TextField(autofillHints: [AutofillHints.username]),
              TextField(autofillHints: [AutofillHints.password], obscureText: true),
            ],
          ),
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}

With a real password manager (seen with <name>, which asks for authentication before filling):
1. Click the username field and pick a saved credential.
2. The password manager shows its authentication prompt; authenticate.
3. Pick the credential again.
4. Only the password appears in the app. The username field stays empty.
5. Reload the page and pick the credential (no authentication prompt this time). Both fields are filled.

Deterministic repro in the DevTools console, which simulates the same sequence:
1. Click the username field.
2. Run:
const u = document.getElementById('username');
const p = document.getElementById('current-password');
window.__u = u;
u.blur(); // window focus loss: relatedTarget == null -> connection closed, form goes dormant
const set = (el, v) => {
  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(HTMLInputElement.prototype, 'value').set.call(el, v);
  el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', {bubbles: true}));
  el.dispatchEvent(new Event('change', {bubbles: true}));
};
set(u, 'user@example.com'); // browser autofills the dormant form
set(p, 'secret');
3. Click the username field again.
4. Run:
({sameUsername: document.getElementById('username') === __u,
  username: document.getElementById('username').value,
  password: document.getElementById('current-password').value})

### Expected results

Both fields show the autofilled values after step 3.

### Actual results

The password field shows secret. The username field is empty. The console prints {sameUsername: false, username: "", password: "secret"}: the username <input> holding the autofilled value was replaced by a new, empty element.

