### Steps to reproduce

1. Run the app below on iOS with Impeller (reproduces on the simulator and on a physical device).
2. Two `BackdropFilter`s with the same blur share one `BackdropGroup`, each under `Opacity(opacity: 0.2)`.

### Expected results

The blur shows at 20 %, so the text under the squares stays mostly sharp, as it does without `backdropGroupKey` or with a single filter in the group.

### Actual results

The blur is drawn at full strength; the `Opacity` has no effect on the backdrop (children of the `BackdropFilter` are still faded). In a real app this shows as glass buttons in a shared group keeping their full blur while they fade out.

The image shows the repro on the unmodified engine (top) and with the fix below (bottom):

<img width="1206" height="1304" alt="backdrop_group_opacity_before_after" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a6e5a350-f1dd-4e20-bd45-dd8e79ded601" />

### Cause

In `Canvas::SaveLayer` (`engine/src/flutter/impeller/display_list/canvas.cc`):

1. `DisplayListBuilder` treats a backdrop save layer as group-opacity compatible, so the enclosing opacity layer gets `can_distribute_opacity`, and Impeller folds its alpha into `distributed_opacity` instead of opening a layer (line 1844).
2. When a `backdrop_id` is used more than once and all filters are equal, the backdrop layer draws the shared filtered snapshot straight into the current pass and returns (line 1976). That `backdrop_entity` gets neither `paint.color.alpha` nor `distributed_opacity`.
3. The regular path absorbs the pending opacity into the layer's paint (line 2016) and applies it on `Restore`, which is why the same tree without `backdropGroupKey` fades correctly.

### Fix

Taking the shared-snapshot shortcut only when the layer is composited opaquely removes the bug in this repro (verified with a local `ios_debug_sim_unopt` build, see the image). A translucent layer falls through to the regular path, which still reads the cached backdrop texture (`backdrop_data->texture_slot`) and applies the opacity on `Restore`, so it costs one filter pass of its own only while its opacity is below 1. The Impeller backdrop unit tests pass on Metal with it; I haven't run the goldens.

```diff
-    if (will_cache_backdrop_texture) {
+    const bool layer_is_opaque =
+        paint.color.alpha * transform_stack_.back().distributed_opacity >=
+        1.0f;
+    if (will_cache_backdrop_texture && layer_is_opaque) {
```

Setting the opacity on the shortcut's `TextureContents` instead would fade the backdrop, but the layer's children would then be faded one by one over it rather than as a group, which differs from the regular path wherever they overlap the backdrop.

