### Result

Profile mode, macOS 27.2.1, Apple M1 Pro, Skia at `f441ca223b2b17b83542ae41d492d0fc76ec1dcb` (framework at a branch from `e71a5b5d8ba`):

```
Paragraph.layout, microseconds for one paragraph of about 500 characters
1. the same paragraph again:                      15.9
2. same word highlighted, another color:          13.3
3. same color, another word highlighted:          152.2
4. a text that was never laid out:                153.7
```

Cases 1 and 2 hit the cache. Case 3 costs the same as a text that was never seen (case 4), about ten times more.

### Expected

Case 3 costs about what cases 1 and 2 cost. The text, the font, the size and every other input of shaping are the same in all three.

### Where it comes from

This is from reading the source, in `modules/skparagraph/src`:

- `ParagraphBuilderImpl::pushStyle` starts a new styled block whenever the style is not fully equal to the last one. A background color is enough. So "plain, highlighted word, plain" is three blocks.
- `ParagraphCacheKey::operator==` (and `computeHash`) compare the number of blocks, and for each block `equalsByFonts`, `fRange.width()` and `fRange.start`. `equalsByFonts` ignores colors, which is why case 2 is a hit. The start and the width of the blocks differ when the highlight is on another word, which is why case 3 is a miss.
- The shaper itself does not need the boundaries: `OneLineShaper::iterateThroughFontStyles` joins neighboring blocks that match in `StyleType::kFont` before it shapes. And what the cache stores (`fRuns`, `fClusters`, the ICU results) is not indexed by block.

So it looks like the key could be built from the blocks after joining neighbors that are `equalsByFonts`, and case 3 would become a hit. I have not tried this.

### Two related limits

They do not belong to this bug, but they decide how much a fix helps the case above:

- The cache holds 128 paragraphs (`kMaxEntries`). A page on which more texts than that get a new highlight in one frame gets little from the cache.
- `isPossiblyTextEditing` does not store a paragraph that begins or ends with the same 40 characters as the paragraph stored last. So one text whose highlight changes is never a hit after the first variant, also for a change of color only. The reproduction with `paragraphCount = 1` prints 12.2, 150.1, 150.7 and 164.3: case 2 is now as slow as case 3.

### Context

Found while comparing two prototypes of text plugins (#65504). In a page of 300 paragraphs of 500 characters where every paragraph gets new search highlights, a CPU profile of the UI thread has `Paragraph.layout` at 44 of 69 ms for one change of the query, with `hb_shape_full` on the stack in 31 % of the samples. The framework also creates a new `ui.Paragraph` for each of these texts, because a paragraph is immutable (#85108), so a cache hit would not make the change free. It would remove the largest part.

