### Expected results

The display turns off when the sensor is covered and the same app process
survives and resumes rendering when the sensor is uncovered.

### Actual results

The issue has been reproduced on a Galaxy A54 (Android 16), Galaxy S22
(Android 16), and Galaxy S23 (Android 17). So far, all confirmed
reproductions have been on Samsung devices. The measurements and log
excerpts below are from the standalone sample on the Galaxy A54.

| Release build | Observed result on the Galaxy A54 |
| --- | --- |
| Impeller enabled (Vulkan) | The app was killed with `LOW_MEMORY` on two runs, 8.8 seconds after the proximity event in each. In the instrumented run, 525 missing buffer-release callbacks for 525 distinct buffer IDs appeared on one `ImpellerSurface` layer after the proximity event. Samsung's `DmaBufLeakDetector` attributed 4,407,040 KiB (about 4.2 GiB) of DMA-BUF RSS to the sample just before the kill. |
| Impeller disabled | The same process survived a 17.5-second covered interval and a second 4.9-second interval, then handled **Stop test**. No missing buffer-release callbacks or low-memory exit were recorded for the process. Global DMA-BUF was 182,560 KiB in the covered samples. |

In the instrumented Impeller run, global DMA-BUF rose from 152,512 KiB
before **Start test** to 473,040 KiB shortly before the sensor triggered,
then to 2,436,160 KiB and 4,459,408 KiB while it was covered. It dropped
to 109,264 KiB in the first sample after the kill. These are global
`dumpsys meminfo --oom` values. The per-process value above comes from
Samsung's `DmaBufLeakDetector`. Three missing-release callbacks were also
logged before the proximity event in this run. The first Impeller run had
28 callbacks before the event and no per-process DMA-BUF measurement.
