### Steps to reproduce

1. On an Android device or emulator using the Gboard spell checker, set the system language to **Español (Estados Unidos)** (Settings > System > Languages).
2. Run the code sample below. It pins the app's locale to English (`MaterialApp.locale: Locale('en')`), as an app with its own language setting does.
3. Tap the text field and type misspelled text, e.g. `Thsi otra palabar`.

Also seen without a locale mismatch: after changing the system language (step 1) and launching an app that follows the device locale, the first request of that launch is sometimes never answered, and spell check stays off until the app restarts. That one is less deterministic; the mismatch above reproduces every time.

### Expected results

Either spell check works in the requested language, or it fails for that request only and works again for later ones (for example after the user changes the system or app language).

### Actual results

No word is ever underlined for the rest of the app session. The first request crashes in the engine's `SpellCheckPlugin`:

- `TextServicesManager.newSpellCheckerSession(null, locale, this, /* referToSpellCheckerLanguageSettings= */ true)` returns `null`, because the requested locale isn't the one enabled in the spell checker settings (documented behaviour of `referToSpellCheckerLanguageSettings`).
- `performSpellCheck` then calls `getSentenceSuggestions` on `null` and throws a `NullPointerException`.
- `initiateSpellCheck` had already stored the request in `pendingResult`, which is never cleared, so **every later request** is answered with `error("Previous spell check request still pending.")`.
- `DefaultSpellCheckService.fetchSpellCheckSuggestions` turns every channel error into `null`, so the app can't see that spell check is permanently off.

The same permanent block happens whenever the platform spell checker never calls back (second case above): there's no timeout, so `pendingResult` stays set.

Related, in the same file: the session is created once, with the first requested locale, and never replaced, so later locale changes (app or system) aren't followed until restart.

This looks like the unresolved cause of #117167, which was closed for lack of a reproduction.

Relevant code (`engine/src/flutter/shell/platform/android/io/flutter/plugin/editing/SpellCheckPlugin.java`, stable 3.50.0):

```java
public void initiateSpellCheck(String locale, String text, MethodChannel.Result result) {
  if (pendingResult != null) {
    result.error("error", "Previous spell check request still pending.", null);
    return;
  }
  pendingResult = result;          // set before the call that can throw
  performSpellCheck(locale, text);
}

public void performSpellCheck(String locale, String text) {
  Locale localeFromString = LocalizationPlugin.localeFromString(locale);
  if (mSpellCheckerSession == null) {
    mSpellCheckerSession = mTextServicesManager.newSpellCheckerSession(
        null, localeFromString, this, /* referToSpellCheckerLanguageSettings= */ true);
  }                                // may still be null here
  TextInfo[] textInfos = new TextInfo[] {new TextInfo(text)};
  mSpellCheckerSession.getSentenceSuggestions(textInfos, MAX_SPELL_CHECK_SUGGESTIONS);  // NPE
}
```

Possible fixes: answer the request with an error and clear `pendingResult` when no session can be created (and on any exception); recreate the session when the requested locale changes; and time out a request that the spell checker never answers.

