### Summary

In `video_player` (`2.14.1`, `flutter/packages` `main` @ `31e61cb00e9813df45db2681c38970e87d30f2e8`), when playing a stream whose reported `value.duration` is `Duration.zero` (such as live streams or streams without container duration), `VideoPlayerController._updatePosition()` erroneously sets `value.isCompleted = true` on the very first 500ms position polling tick—even though playback is actively running and `VideoEventType.completed` has not occurred.

### Root Cause

In [`packages/video_player/video_player/lib/video_player.dart#L980-L996`](https://github.com/flutter/packages/blob/31e61cb00e9813df45db2681c38970e87d30f2e8/packages/video_player/video_player/lib/video_player.dart#L980-L996):

```dart
void _updatePosition(Duration position) {
  // A pending platform seek or position request can complete after disposal.
  if (_isDisposed) {
    return;
  }
  // The underlying native implementation on some platforms sometimes reports
  // a position slightly past the reported max duration. Clamp to the duration
  // to insulate clients from this behavior.
  if (position > value.duration) {
    position = value.duration;
  }
  value = value.copyWith(
    position: position,
    caption: _getCaptionAt(position),
    isCompleted: position == value.duration,
  );
}
```

When `value.duration == Duration.zero`:
1. Playback starts with `position == Duration.zero`, `isPlaying == true`, `isCompleted == false`.
2. After 500ms, the periodic timer fetches `newPosition` (e.g., `500ms`) and calls `_updatePosition(500ms)`.
3. `position > value.duration` (`500ms > 0ms`) clamps `position` back to `Duration.zero`.
4. `isCompleted: position == value.duration` (`Duration.zero == Duration.zero`) evaluates to **`true`**.

*(Note: A closely related symptom also happens when `C.TIME_UNSET` overflows to `1ms`—see #197492 and #102366—where `500ms > 1ms` likewise clamps to `1ms` and flips `isCompleted` to `true` after 500ms.)*

### Expected Behavior

- For streams with `value.duration == Duration.zero` (unknown / indeterminate duration), position polling should **not** infer `isCompleted = true` merely because `position >= Duration.zero`.
- Completion for such streams should only be set when the platform explicitly emits `VideoEventType.completed`.

### Actual Behavior

- `controller.value.isCompleted` becomes `true` ~500ms after playback begins while video/audio are still playing.
- Any app listener reacting to `value.isCompleted` (e.g. auto-resetting to start or advancing to the next playlist item) immediately interrupts playback after 0.5 seconds.

### Proposed Fix

Only derive `isCompleted` from position comparison when `value.duration > Duration.zero`:

```dart
final bool hasKnownDuration = value.duration > Duration.zero;
if (hasKnownDuration && position > value.duration) {
  position = value.duration;
}
value = value.copyWith(
  position: position,
  caption: _getCaptionAt(position),
  isCompleted: hasKnownDuration && position == value.duration,
);
```

*(Also worth checking `play()` where `if (value.position == value.duration) await seekTo(Duration.zero);` triggers an unnecessary `seekTo(0)` when resuming a paused zero-duration stream.)*

Happy to submit a PR with unit tests once maintainers confirm the desired behavior.

**Description**

When using Flutter's default iOS SystemContextMenu implementation, tapping the native Select All action (IOSSystemContextMenuItemDataSelectAll) correctly selects all text in an editable text field. However, after the action is performed:

1. The text selection handles unexpectedly disappear.
2. The native context menu is dismissed.

This makes it impossible to perform subsequent selection-related actions without interacting with the text field again.

Switching manually to AdaptiveTextSelectionToolbar resolves the issue but only partially. When you copy some text from **outside** the app and try to paste it, the clipboard dialog should appear which asks for permission to paste the content. It works fine on iOS < 27, but doesn't work on iOS 27 and higher. However, everything works correctly when the text is copied **within** the app

**Suspected cause**

The issue might be related to how native iOS selection changes are propagated back to Flutter.

The native selectAll: implementation updates the selected text range through setSelectedTextRange:, which subsequently triggers updateEditingState.

Flutter processes this platform-originated selection update in EditableTextState._updateEditingValue, apparently using **SelectionChangedCause.keyboard**.

This differs from a selection initiated through Flutter's own selection mechanisms, where SelectionChangedCause.toolbar or SelectionChangedCause.longPress may be used.


**Workaround**

Replacing IOSSystemContextMenuItemDataSelectAll with IOSSystemContextMenuItemCustom and performing selection through EditableTextState.userUpdateTextEditingValue() appears to bypass the native selection-update path.

### Expected results

1. Tapping Select All should select the entire text.
2. Selection handles should remain visible.
3. Subsequent selection actions (Copy, Cut, etc.) should remain accessible.

### Actual results


1. Text is successfully selected.
2. Selection handles disappear.
3. Subsequent selection actions (Copy, Cut, etc.) just not being displayed until I tap on the text again.

