### Steps to reproduce

1. Show a `ListWheelScrollView` with `overAndUnderCenterOpacity` below 1. Every `CupertinoPicker` does this (it uses 0.447).
2. Scroll it.
3. Set `overAndUnderCenterOpacity` to 1, or remove all children.

The code sample below shows this with `flutter test`.

### Expected results

The viewport reuses its `OpacityLayer` across repaints, like it already does for its clip layer, and releases it once it no longer needs it.

### Actual results

`pushOpacity` is called without `oldLayer` ([L832](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/6e582bb058d6a82c7660537be48fecae8b213331/packages/flutter/lib/src/rendering/list_wheel_viewport.dart#L832)), so every paint creates a new `OpacityLayer`. The handle added in #136576 is also not cleared when the opacity becomes 1 ([L825](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/6e582bb058d6a82c7660537be48fecae8b213331/packages/flutter/lib/src/rendering/list_wheel_viewport.dart#L825)) or when there are no children ([L794](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/6e582bb058d6a82c7660537be48fecae8b213331/packages/flutter/lib/src/rendering/list_wheel_viewport.dart#L794)). The detached layer and its children stay alive until the opacity drops below 1 again, or until the render object is disposed.

In a profile build with four `CupertinoPicker`s scrolling, that's 240 new `OpacityLayer`s every 60 frames instead of 4. I didn't see a frame-time difference on Linux desktop with software rendering, so this is about layer reuse and memory, not speed.

